  "query_preview": "../Friday through Monday/...A front moving eastward \ninto the panhandle will continue to spread inland into the \nsouthern panhandle through Friday morning. Rain will start to \ntaper off from north to south through Friday and into Saturday, \nthough continued onshore flow will keep the potential for showers \nelevated through the weekend. Rainfall rates may be heavier with \nthe initial onset of the front, with the indication for a plume of\nmoisture being directed into the central and southern panhandle. \nBehind the initial front, light rainfall rates should take hold \nfor the rest of the period, though more developed showers may \nbring periods of heavier rainfall and gusty winds as they blow \nover. \n\nWinds will increase for parts of the N/S facing inner channels \nwith the front, though expecting speeds to remain around 15 to 20 \nmph and decrease into the weekend. The strongest speeds are \nexpected through Northern Lynn Canal and into Taiya Inlet, which \nmay be accentuated by any light sea breezing through the \nafternoon. These winds have been increased to 30 to 35 mph through\nThursday, but should decrease down to 20 to 25 mph and continue \nto drop through the weekend. As the low dips south through the \nweekend, winds in the northern panhandle may turn offshore Sunday \nnight.\n\nAs this low dips southward through the weekend, easterly flow \nindicates potential for Thunderstorms in BC to attempt to make it \nover the Coastal Range and into the panhandle. While it is still \ntoo far out to decide if this will be a strong enough pattern, \nthis will continue to be looked at through the week. High \ntemperatures will be capped around the low to mid 60s with the \nincreased cloud cover and active weather in the forecast.",
